Show snouty's settings, and trust or deny a project settings file

`settings show` prints the settings that snouty resolved, from the environment,
the project settings file and the global settings file.

A project settings file (./.snouty.toml) can set `base_url` and `https_proxy`,
which decide where snouty sends your credentials, and a repository that you
clone can contain one. So snouty uses ./.snouty.toml only after you trust it
with `settings trust`. When the file changes, you must trust it again. To make
snouty ignore the file, use `settings deny`. Until you do one of the two, each
command that reads the settings fails.

snouty trusts a file that you name with --settings or SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH, and
the global settings file. Set SNOUTY_SETTINGS_TRUST=all to use every project
settings file without a check, for example in CI.

`settings show` prints JSON with --json.

Usage: snouty settings [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  show   Print the settings that snouty resolved
  trust  Use a project settings file while its content stays the same
  deny   Ignore a project settings file
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --json
          Output JSON where supported (NDJSON for list/stream commands, pretty
          JSON otherwise)

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
